1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Have you ever met a transgender
person? Maybe you have.

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:06,700
Maybe you think you haven't.
But how do you know?

3
00:00:07,225 --> 00:00:10,785
A transgender person doesn't
necessarily look a certain way.

4
00:00:11,465 --> 00:00:16,265
They don't have a particular personality,
voice, job, or sexual orientation.

5
00:00:17,179 --> 00:00:21,299
Some are very obviously
transgender. Others aren't.

6
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:23,419
Some talk openly about it.

7
00:00:23,639 --> 00:00:25,339
Others never tell anyone.

8
00:00:25,543 --> 00:00:28,983
And some people may spend
their entire lives hiding it.

9
00:00:29,279 --> 00:00:32,059
So perhaps the first
question isn't: "Why are

10
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:34,779
there suddenly so many
transgender people?"

11
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:39,092
Perhaps the first question should be:
who are transgender people, anyway?

12
00:00:39,392 --> 00:00:41,152
Let's take a closer look.

13
00:00:43,352 --> 00:00:45,672
What does transgender actually mean?

14
00:00:46,371 --> 00:00:49,151
When a baby is born,
doctors usually assign a sex

15
00:00:49,381 --> 00:00:52,411
based primarily on the baby's
physical characteristics.

16
00:00:53,357 --> 00:00:55,197
For most people, the
sex they were assigned

17
00:00:55,427 --> 00:00:58,237
at birth and their gender
identity correspond.

18
00:00:59,077 --> 00:01:02,217
These people are commonly
described as cisgender.

19
00:01:02,842 --> 00:01:05,202
For some people, however, they don't.

20
00:01:05,662 --> 00:01:09,522
Their gender identity differs from
the sex they were assigned at birth.

21
00:01:10,262 --> 00:01:14,422
These people may describe themselves
as transgender, or simply trans.

22
00:01:14,809 --> 00:01:16,809
And this distinction is important.

23
00:01:17,669 --> 00:01:19,569
Being transgender is not the same thing as

24
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:23,009
being gay, lesbian,
bisexual, or heterosexual.

25
00:01:23,403 --> 00:01:27,103
Gender identity and sexual
orientation are different things.

26
00:01:27,943 --> 00:01:29,563
One is about who you are.

27
00:01:29,983 --> 00:01:32,403
The other is about who
you're attracted to.

28
00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:36,155
A trans woman is a woman who
was assigned male at birth.

29
00:01:37,035 --> 00:01:39,975
In other words, she was born
with a typically male body.

30
00:01:40,635 --> 00:01:45,395
A trans man is the other way round: a
man who was assigned female at birth.

31
00:01:45,667 --> 00:01:50,147
A trans woman can be heterosexual,
lesbian, bisexual, or asexual.

32
00:01:50,727 --> 00:01:55,107
A trans man can be heterosexual,
gay, bisexual, or asexual.

33
00:01:55,770 --> 00:02:00,430
There is no particular sexual orientation
that comes with being transgender.

34
00:02:01,199 --> 00:02:04,679
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

35
00:02:05,299 --> 00:02:06,919
It really helps the channel.

36
00:02:09,089 --> 00:02:11,249
But what does it actually feel like?

37
00:02:11,937 --> 00:02:14,797
This is where things become
difficult to explain.

38
00:02:15,617 --> 00:02:17,917
Because if you've never
experienced it yourself,

39
00:02:18,197 --> 00:02:21,137
you may simply have no
idea what it feels like.

40
00:02:21,571 --> 00:02:25,651
Some transgender people experience
something called gender dysphoria.

41
00:02:25,897 --> 00:02:28,897
It can involve significant
distress related to a mismatch

42
00:02:29,127 --> 00:02:32,477
between a person's experienced
gender and aspects of

43
00:02:32,707 --> 00:02:36,337
their body, or the way they
are perceived by other people.

44
00:02:36,494 --> 00:02:39,654
And this is not simply:
"I don't like my body."

45
00:02:40,194 --> 00:02:42,634
Many people don't like
parts of their bodies.

46
00:02:43,019 --> 00:02:45,979
Someone can hate their nose,
their weight, their hair,

47
00:02:46,059 --> 00:02:49,179
or their height without
experiencing gender dysphoria.

48
00:02:49,879 --> 00:02:53,119
Gender dysphoria is about
something more fundamental.

49
00:02:53,805 --> 00:02:56,845
For some people, particular parts
of their body can feel profoundly

50
00:02:57,075 --> 00:03:01,125
wrong, because they don't fit the
way they experience themselves.

51
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,290
Being called by the
wrong name or pronoun can

52
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:07,110
also be deeply distressing
for some people.

53
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:09,730
Looking in the mirror can be difficult.

54
00:03:10,532 --> 00:03:13,532
Puberty can be especially
difficult, because the body

55
00:03:13,762 --> 00:03:16,552
begins developing in a direction
that feels increasingly

56
00:03:16,782 --> 00:03:20,392
incompatible with how the
person understands themselves.

57
00:03:20,895 --> 00:03:24,995
And this experience can be difficult for
someone who has never had it to imagine.

58
00:03:25,348 --> 00:03:29,128
Imagine waking up every morning and
seeing something about your body that

59
00:03:29,358 --> 00:03:33,628
doesn't merely look unpleasant to you,
but feels fundamentally out of place.

60
00:03:33,997 --> 00:03:38,157
That analogy will never perfectly
reproduce another person's experience.

61
00:03:38,477 --> 00:03:43,337
But it may help explain why simply
saying: "Just accept your body."

62
00:03:43,517 --> 00:03:46,097
doesn't necessarily solve the problem.

63
00:03:46,348 --> 00:03:49,828
And importantly, not every
transgender person experiences

64
00:03:50,058 --> 00:03:54,308
gender dysphoria in exactly the
same way, or to the same degree.

65
00:03:54,556 --> 00:03:56,936
Some people experience severe distress.

66
00:03:57,016 --> 00:04:00,116
Others describe a more
persistent sense that

67
00:04:00,346 --> 00:04:02,496
something about their gender doesn't fit.

68
00:04:02,836 --> 00:04:07,916
And some may identify as transgender
without seeking medical treatment at all.

69
00:04:10,506 --> 00:04:13,126
There isn't one kind
of transgender person.

70
00:04:13,781 --> 00:04:16,241
This is another thing
that's easy to forget.

71
00:04:16,901 --> 00:04:19,481
Transgender people aren't a uniform group.

72
00:04:19,821 --> 00:04:21,501
They can be young or old.

73
00:04:22,158 --> 00:04:24,218
They can be religious or not.

74
00:04:24,678 --> 00:04:26,518
They can be married, with children.

75
00:04:27,198 --> 00:04:29,858
They can be politically
conservative or progressive.

76
00:04:30,706 --> 00:04:35,686
Some want hormones. Some don't.
Some want surgery. Some don't.

77
00:04:35,766 --> 00:04:37,686
Some change their name and appearance.

78
00:04:38,045 --> 00:04:39,385
Some don't.

79
00:04:39,507 --> 00:04:42,007
And some simply want to live
their lives without making

80
00:04:42,237 --> 00:04:46,067
their gender identity a major
part of their public identity.

81
00:04:46,807 --> 00:04:49,407
There is no single transgender experience.

82
00:04:51,977 --> 00:04:53,177
What is transition?

83
00:04:54,117 --> 00:04:56,737
The word transition
can also be misleading.

84
00:04:57,557 --> 00:05:01,757
People sometimes imagine that transition
means one particular operation.

85
00:05:02,677 --> 00:05:04,637
It doesn't.

86
00:05:04,867 --> 00:05:06,837
Transition can involve
several different things.

87
00:05:07,595 --> 00:05:09,495
There can be social transition.

88
00:05:10,295 --> 00:05:14,435
This can mean changing someone's name,
pronouns, clothing, or appearance.

89
00:05:14,802 --> 00:05:17,842
There can be legal or
administrative transition.

90
00:05:18,682 --> 00:05:20,882
This can mean changing
the name or gender marker

91
00:05:21,112 --> 00:05:24,242
on official documents,
where the law allows it.

92
00:05:24,356 --> 00:05:26,456
And there can be medical transition.

93
00:05:27,376 --> 00:05:31,576
This can include hormone therapy and,
for some people, surgical procedures.

94
00:05:32,197 --> 00:05:34,617
But these are not three mandatory steps.

95
00:05:35,357 --> 00:05:38,577
A transgender person does not
have to complete all of them.

96
00:05:39,121 --> 00:05:41,081
There isn't a checklist
that someone has to

97
00:05:41,311 --> 00:05:44,261
complete before their
identity becomes "real."

98
00:05:44,635 --> 00:05:47,615
And some people decide
not to transition at all.

99
00:05:48,135 --> 00:05:49,995
They keep these feelings to themselves.

100
00:05:50,605 --> 00:05:53,805
They may get married.
They may have children.

101
00:05:53,905 --> 00:05:55,965
They may build a career.

102
00:05:56,285 --> 00:06:00,725
They may appear to everyone around them
to be living a completely ordinary life.

103
00:06:01,016 --> 00:06:04,036
So the person sitting next to
you at work, or someone you've

104
00:06:04,266 --> 00:06:08,056
known for twenty years, may have
a story you know nothing about.

105
00:06:08,264 --> 00:06:11,384
And that's not necessarily
because they're deceiving you.

106
00:06:11,864 --> 00:06:14,904
It may simply be because their
gender history is private.

107
00:06:17,874 --> 00:06:20,054
Is being transgender a fashion?

108
00:06:20,786 --> 00:06:23,926
It's certainly true that young
people influence each other.

109
00:06:24,466 --> 00:06:28,346
It's also true that social media
can spread ideas very quickly.

110
00:06:29,126 --> 00:06:32,386
And people can sometimes
misinterpret their own feelings.

111
00:06:32,895 --> 00:06:34,475
But that's different
from saying that being

112
00:06:34,705 --> 00:06:37,335
transgender is simply a fashion trend.

113
00:06:37,962 --> 00:06:42,262
For some people, these feelings appear
to be persistent and deeply significant.

114
00:06:43,042 --> 00:06:45,402
Some spend years trying to suppress them.

115
00:06:45,862 --> 00:06:48,182
Some risk rejection from their families.

116
00:06:48,606 --> 00:06:52,466
Some risk losing relationships,
jobs, or social connections.

117
00:06:53,466 --> 00:06:57,126
Some eventually decide that living
openly is worth those risks.

118
00:06:57,477 --> 00:06:59,537
That doesn't mean that
every person who questions

119
00:06:59,767 --> 00:07:02,717
their gender is necessarily transgender.

120
00:07:03,737 --> 00:07:08,077
It doesn't mean that every decision about
transition is automatically correct.

121
00:07:08,937 --> 00:07:12,217
And it doesn't mean that
nobody ever changes their mind.

122
00:07:12,873 --> 00:07:16,913
It means that the simple explanation,
"They're just doing it because it's

123
00:07:17,143 --> 00:07:21,033
fashionable," is probably not enough
to explain the whole phenomenon.

124
00:07:21,716 --> 00:07:25,536
Human biology is not a
perfectly standardised system.

125
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:28,456
People are born with
countless variations in

126
00:07:28,686 --> 00:07:31,596
their bodies, brains,
development, and senses.

127
00:07:32,331 --> 00:07:34,111
Some are obvious from birth.

128
00:07:34,891 --> 00:07:38,471
Others aren't discovered until
childhood, puberty, or adulthood.

129
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:42,680
So why would we assume that gender
identity must be an exception?

130
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:45,720
Why would every person's
brain and body have

131
00:07:45,950 --> 00:07:48,400
to fit together in exactly the same way?

132
00:07:48,910 --> 00:07:51,410
Perhaps the more surprising
assumption is not that

133
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:54,370
some people experience
their gender differently.

134
00:07:55,130 --> 00:07:58,390
Perhaps it's the assumption that
this could never happen at all.

135
00:07:58,649 --> 00:08:01,629
Human beings are more
complicated than that.

136
00:08:04,009 --> 00:08:06,749
Are there really more
transgender people now?

137
00:08:07,348 --> 00:08:09,228
It certainly looks that way.

138
00:08:10,068 --> 00:08:13,328
In the United States, researchers
estimate that about one in

139
00:08:13,558 --> 00:08:17,008
a hundred people aged thirteen
and over describe themselves as

140
00:08:17,238 --> 00:08:21,708
transgender, a group that also
includes many non-binary people.

141
00:08:21,873 --> 00:08:24,913
But that number hides an
enormous difference between ages.

142
00:08:25,953 --> 00:08:30,880
Among people aged sixty-five and
over, it's about one in four hundred.

143
00:08:31,013 --> 00:08:32,713
Among young adults aged eighteen to

144
00:08:32,943 --> 00:08:35,637
twenty-four, it's nearly
three in a hundred.

145
00:08:35,717 --> 00:08:37,997
That's roughly ten times as many.

146
00:08:38,471 --> 00:08:41,191
So are young people simply
being born differently?

147
00:08:41,711 --> 00:08:43,531
These numbers don't show that.

148
00:08:43,759 --> 00:08:46,019
But there is an important
distinction between how

149
00:08:46,249 --> 00:08:49,459
many people exist and how
many people are visible.

150
00:08:50,105 --> 00:08:53,805
A young person today can search
the internet, find other people

151
00:08:54,035 --> 00:08:57,365
describing almost exactly the
same feelings, and discover

152
00:08:57,595 --> 00:09:00,965
words for experiences they
couldn't previously explain.

153
00:09:01,636 --> 00:09:05,476
Someone born in the 1950s
grew up without any of that.

154
00:09:06,041 --> 00:09:08,481
The numbers alone can't tell
us how much of the difference

155
00:09:08,711 --> 00:09:12,341
comes from visibility, and
how much from something else.

156
00:09:12,707 --> 00:09:16,127
And this isn't the first time people
like this have become visible.

157
00:09:18,947 --> 00:09:21,547
Were transgender people
around in the past?

158
00:09:22,307 --> 00:09:27,607
In 1919, a doctor called Magnus
Hirschfeld opened an institute in Berlin.

159
00:09:28,355 --> 00:09:31,995
It studied human sexuality and
gender, including people who

160
00:09:32,225 --> 00:09:35,255
felt that the sex they had
been assigned didn't fit them.

161
00:09:35,655 --> 00:09:39,075
It built up a large
library on sex and gender.

162
00:09:39,759 --> 00:09:44,319
In 1930, a Danish painter called
Lili Elbe came to the institute.

163
00:09:45,253 --> 00:09:49,093
Over the following sixteen months,
she underwent four operations.

164
00:09:49,913 --> 00:09:52,453
She became one of the
best-known early cases of

165
00:09:52,683 --> 00:09:55,253
what we would now call
gender-affirming surgery.

166
00:09:55,973 --> 00:10:01,353
She died in 1931, most likely from
complications after the last one.

167
00:10:01,886 --> 00:10:06,246
Two years later, in May 1933,
the institute was raided.

168
00:10:06,553 --> 00:10:10,613
A few days later, much of its library
was burned on a public square.

169
00:10:11,299 --> 00:10:13,119
Almost a hundred years
ago, there was already

170
00:10:13,349 --> 00:10:15,719
a place where people
like this could be seen.

171
00:10:16,459 --> 00:10:18,959
And then, quite suddenly, there wasn't.

172
00:10:21,939 --> 00:10:23,759
Berlin wasn't unique.

173
00:10:24,379 --> 00:10:26,439
Many cultures have had their
own ways of understanding

174
00:10:26,669 --> 00:10:29,939
people who didn't fit
conventional gender roles.

175
00:10:30,256 --> 00:10:34,916
In South Asia, there are the Hijra,
communities with centuries of history.

176
00:10:35,856 --> 00:10:38,736
In North America, some
Indigenous nations have roles

177
00:10:38,966 --> 00:10:41,456
now often described by the
modern term Two-Spirit.

178
00:10:42,285 --> 00:10:44,825
But people whose lives didn't
fit conventional gender

179
00:10:45,055 --> 00:10:49,165
expectations are certainly not
a new invention of the internet.

180
00:10:51,815 --> 00:10:54,395
So why do we notice them so much now?

181
00:10:55,004 --> 00:10:58,364
Partly because, in some parts
of the world, people can now

182
00:10:58,594 --> 00:11:01,964
talk openly about experiences
that used to stay hidden.

183
00:11:02,124 --> 00:11:05,864
So when more people say, "I
am transgender," that doesn't

184
00:11:06,094 --> 00:11:08,924
automatically mean that more
people became transgender.

185
00:11:09,751 --> 00:11:12,211
It can also mean that
more people are visible.

186
00:11:12,291 --> 00:11:15,691
And visibility is not the
same thing as existence.

187
00:11:18,621 --> 00:11:21,421
Maybe the real change isn't in the people.

188
00:11:22,087 --> 00:11:26,767
For centuries, many people who didn't
fit society's expectations hid it.

189
00:11:27,307 --> 00:11:31,647
They lived in a role expected of
them, or simply never told anyone.

190
00:11:32,191 --> 00:11:35,471
Today, in at least some parts of the
world, there are more possibilities.

191
00:11:35,811 --> 00:11:38,991
But that doesn't mean everyone takes them.

192
00:11:39,171 --> 00:11:42,771
And it certainly doesn't mean
that being transgender is easy.

193
00:11:45,841 --> 00:11:48,981
In fact, the next question
may be even more interesting.

194
00:11:49,881 --> 00:11:53,041
How does a person actually figure
out that they are transgender?

195
00:11:53,411 --> 00:11:55,991
Because some people
seem to know very early.

196
00:11:56,671 --> 00:11:59,611
Others spend years wondering
what is wrong with them.

197
00:11:59,743 --> 00:12:02,043
Some are convinced that
they're simply failing to be

198
00:12:02,273 --> 00:12:06,083
the kind of man or woman
society expects them to be.

199
00:12:06,543 --> 00:12:10,403
And some only begin to understand
themselves decades later.

200
00:12:10,599 --> 00:12:13,859
So if being transgender isn't
simply a fashion choice...

201
00:12:13,979 --> 00:12:16,439
how does someone actually discover it?

202
00:12:17,009 --> 00:12:18,689
That's what we'll look at next.

203
00:12:19,489 --> 00:12:20,959
How do you know you're trans?
